## Appendix B. Regulatory quick reference

Use this as a quick reference. It is not legal advice; refer specific legal questions to the Lead Auditor or Legal Reviewer.

| Requirement | HIPAA (US healthcare) | EU AI Act | GDPR (EU) | India DPDP Act |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Applies to | Covered entities and business associates processing PHI in the US | Any organization placing AI systems on the EU market or affecting EU persons | Any organization processing personal data of EU residents | Any organization processing personal data of Indian residents |
| Data consent or legal basis | Authorization for non-TPO uses; TPO uses do not require consent | Transparency required; consent for GPAI training in some cases | Explicit legal basis required (consent, contract, legitimate interest and others) | Explicit consent for most processing; certain legitimate uses exempt |
| Bias and fairness testing | Not explicitly required but implied by non-discrimination laws | Mandatory for all high-risk AI systems | Non-discrimination principle applies | Fairness principle in the India AI Guidelines; mandatory for SDFs under DPDP |
| Human oversight | Required for clinical decisions; not a universal mandate | Mandatory for all high-risk AI: meaningful human review | Right not to be subject to solely automated decisions (Art. 22) | Human oversight principle in the AI Guidelines; human in the loop for high-impact uses |
| Model documentation | Not specifically mandated | Technical documentation and model cards mandatory for high-risk AI | DPIAs required for high-risk processing | DPIAs required for Significant Data Fiduciaries; AI impact assessments evolving |
| Incident notification | 60 days for breaches affecting 500 or more individuals; annual report for smaller breaches | Serious incident reporting to the EU AI Office for high-risk AI providers | 72 hours to the supervisory authority for personal data breaches | Breach reporting timelines specified in the DPDP Rules 2025 |
| Penalties | Up to $2M per violation category; criminal penalties for wilful neglect | Up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited AI practices | Up to €20M or 4% of global turnover | Up to INR 250 crore under DPDP; AI-specific penalties emerging |
| Audit or assessment required | HIPAA risk analysis at least annually or when the environment changes | Conformity assessment for high-risk AI before market placement | DPIAs for high-risk processing; ongoing review | DPIAs for SDFs; IndiaAI Safety Institute audits evolving |
| Explainability required | Not explicitly mandated (adverse action notices required in BFSI) | Mandatory for high-risk AI: decisions must be explainable | Right to explanation for automated decisions (Art. 22) | Transparency principle in the AI Guidelines; explanation emerging |
| Template reference | BAA checklist (§9); HIPAA assessment (§5.3) | EU AI Act risk classification (§3.3); bias testing (§6.2) | Data privacy fields throughout §5 | DPDP consent fields (§5.2); India framework (§4) |

## Appendix C. Glossary

| Term | Plain English definition | Why it matters in an AI audit |
|---|---|---|
| Adversarial Attack | A deliberate input designed to trick an AI model into making an incorrect prediction | If the model is used in security, fraud detection or medical diagnosis, adversarial robustness is critical |
| Algorithm | A set of rules or instructions that a computer follows to make a decision or prediction | All AI systems are built on algorithms; understanding what the algorithm does is the starting point of any audit |
| Agentic AI | AI that can take actions on its own (browse the web, run code, send emails) without human approval for each step | Much higher risk than advisory AI; human oversight controls are especially important |
| BAA (Business Associate Agreement) | A HIPAA-required contract between a healthcare organization and any vendor that handles patient health information on its behalf | Without a signed BAA, using a vendor for AI on PHI is a HIPAA violation |
| Bias (Algorithmic) | When an AI model produces systematically different (worse) outcomes for one demographic group compared with another | Can cause illegal discrimination in credit, hiring, insurance or healthcare; must be tested before and during deployment |
| Data Drift | When the real-world data an AI system meets in production starts to differ from the data it was trained on, so performance degrades | Left untreated, the model becomes less accurate over time without anyone noticing |
| Data Fiduciary | Under India's DPDP Act, any entity that determines the purpose and means of processing personal data | Equivalent to a data controller under GDPR; has specific compliance obligations |
| DPIA (Data Protection Impact Assessment) | A formal analysis of the privacy risks of a processing activity, required before high-risk processing begins | Required by GDPR and evolving under India DPDP for high-risk AI systems |
| Disparate Impact | When a neutral-looking policy or algorithm produces different outcomes for different protected groups | Measured with the four-fifths rule: a selection rate for the less-favored group below 80% of the favored group's rate indicates disparate impact |
| Explainability (XAI) | The ability to describe in plain language why an AI model made a particular prediction or decision | Required by the EU AI Act for high-risk AI and by GDPR Art. 22 for automated decisions; needed for consumer adverse action notices |
| Feature | An input variable a model uses to make predictions, such as income, age, zip code or diagnosis code | Some features are proxies for protected characteristics (zip code correlates with race), creating indirect bias |
| Foundation Model (LLM) | A large pre-trained AI model that can be adapted for many tasks | Governed under the EU AI Act GPAI provisions; prompt injection and output filtering are key security controls |
| GenAI (Generative AI) | AI that generates new content (text, images, audio, code) rather than only classifying or predicting | Synthetic media labeling requirements apply (EU AI Act, India IT Amendment Rules 2026) |
| High-Risk AI (EU AI Act) | AI systems used where errors can significantly harm people: healthcare, credit, employment, education, law enforcement | Subject to the strictest EU AI Act obligations: conformity assessment, documentation, human oversight, registration |
| HIPAA | US law protecting patient health information; requires covered entities and business associates to protect PHI | Any AI touching US health data must comply; BAAs are required with all vendors processing PHI |
| Human-in-the-Loop | A design in which a human reviews and approves AI outputs before they are acted on | Required by the EU AI Act for high-risk systems; prevents fully automated harmful decisions |
| Model Card | A short document describing what a model does, how it was trained, how it performs and its limitations | Required for high-risk AI under the EU AI Act and good practice for all models |
| Model Drift | When a model's predictive accuracy declines over time because the real world has changed | Requires continuous monitoring and periodic retraining; a model with no monitoring is a liability |
| Model Risk | The risk of adverse consequences from decisions based on poorly developed, implemented or used AI models | Formally managed in banking (OCC SR 11-7); an emerging requirement across industries |
| Model Validation | An independent review confirming a model is conceptually sound, performs as intended and suits its use case | Must be done by someone not involved in building the model; required in banking, evolving elsewhere |
| PHI (Protected Health Information) | Under HIPAA, any individually identifiable health information, including names, dates, diagnoses, insurance numbers and images | The most sensitive data category in US healthcare AI; any AI touching PHI needs rigorous HIPAA compliance |
| PII (Personally Identifiable Information) | Any data that could identify an individual: name, address, SSN, email, IP address, biometric data | Triggers privacy law obligations under GDPR, DPDP and state laws |
| Prompt Injection | An attack in which malicious text in an input causes a GenAI system to behave in unintended ways | A critical security risk for GenAI systems; must be tested and mitigated before deployment |
| SHAP and LIME | Mathematical tools that explain which features most influenced a model's prediction in a specific case | Their use shows the organization is thinking about explainability |
| SDF (Significant Data Fiduciary) | Under India's DPDP Act, a data fiduciary classified as handling especially sensitive or large volumes of data | Subject to additional obligations, including DPIAs, audits and appointing a Data Protection Officer |
| SOC 2 Type II | An independent audit confirming a vendor's security, availability and privacy controls exist and worked over 6–12 months | Expected evidence from high-risk AI vendors; Type II (tested over time) is stronger than Type I (point in time) |
| Training Data | The dataset used to teach an AI model to make predictions | Its quality, bias, legal basis and provenance are the foundation of the audit |
